For minimizing memory space, a hash table may not always be the best option. While a hash table can offer O(1) average case time complexity for search, delete, and insert operations, it may require more memory space than a balanced binary search tree. A hash table needs additional memory for its underlying array, which can lead to memory wastage especially when the hash table is not filled up to its full capacity. On the other hand, a balanced binary search tree optimizes memory usage by maintaining a balanced structure, which can save memory space compared to a hash table. Therefore, in terms of memory space optimization, the implementation with a balanced binary search tree may be preferable over a hash table.

