    \item The maximum cost of a union operation occurs when the condition for "enforcement" is met, i.e., \( \frac{\text{size}(A)}{\text{size}(B)} > 5 \) or \( \frac{\text{size}(A)}{\text{size}(B)} < \frac{1}{5} \). In this case, the shorter list (either \( A \) or \( B \)) is merged into the longer one. Therefore, the maximum cost would be equal to the size of the shorter list. The worst-case scenario is when the two lists have nearly equal sizes, and the size of the shorter list is around half of the larger list. So, the maximum cost of a union operation is approximately \( \frac{n}{2} \), where \( n \) is the total number of elements in the two lists.

    \item The complexity of \( n \) union operations will depend on the number of elements in each list and how they are merged. Since the maximum cost of a union operation is \( \frac{n}{2} \), and we perform \( n \) union operations, the overall complexity of \( n \) union operations would be \( O(n^2) \) in the worst case scenario. This is because each union operation may potentially iterate through almost all the elements in the two lists, resulting in a quadratic complexity.
\end{enumerate}

